
How to set up the server
------------------------

1. Create a Python environment (this step is optional)
My preferred tool to manage Python environments is conda-forge.
First, install conda by running the Miniforge installer
(see https://conda-forge.org).
Then, create and activate an environment for the cgos server:
conda create -n cgos-server python==3.12.0
conda activate cgos-server

2. Go to directory <server-code>
cd <server-code>

3. Install dependencies
pip install -r requirements.txt

4. Configure cgos19.cfg
You have to decide where on the server the data of the running server
should be written. I will refer to this directory as <server-data>.
You must as well decide where on the server the web pages of the server
should be stored. I will refer to this directory as <server-html>.
Edit cgos19.cfg and replace all occurrences of <server-data> with
your (absolute) path. Do the same with <server-html>.
Note: <server-html> should be a directory that your web server presents
to client browsers. In my case (Ubuntu 24.04), the web server is Apache2,
configured to use /var/www/html as base directory. Thus, <server-html>
is /var/www/html/cgos on the machine running the server.

5. Copy the directory tree used for presentation of results
mkdir -p <server-html>
cp -rp cgos/webuild_html/* <server-html>

6. Start the Go server
python cgos/server.py cgos19.cfg &

7. Start the webpage builder
python cgos/webuild.py cgos19.cfg &
Note: You should wait with this until the first match at least has
begun, otherwise the start will fail and you have to restart later.

8. Stop the Go server
touch <server-code>/kill_cgos19
The kill file is automatically deleted when the server is started again.

9. Stop the webpage builder
touch <server-code>/kill_webuild
The kill file is automatically deleted when the builder is started again.

